---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Example from '../../components/Example.astro';
import { componentsSidebar } from '../../data/nav';
import groupHtml from '../../examples/checkbox/group.html?raw';
import singleHtml from '../../examples/checkbox/single.html?raw';
import disabledHtml from '../../examples/checkbox/disabled.html?raw';

const onThisPage = [
  { label: 'Preview', href: '#preview' },
  { label: 'When to use', href: '#when-to-use' },
  { label: 'When not to use', href: '#when-not-to-use' },
  { label: 'Guidance', href: '#guidance' },
  { label: 'Single checkbox', href: '#single-checkbox' },
  { label: 'Disabled checkboxes', href: '#disabled-checkboxes' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title="Checkbox"
  section="components"
  description="Use checkboxes to let users select one or more options from a list."
  pageTitle="Checkbox"
  pageSubtitle="Checkboxes let users select any number of options from a list, including none."
  sidebarGroups={componentsSidebar}
  sidebarLabel="Components"
  onThisPage={onThisPage}
>
  <div class="govbb-prose">
    <h2 id="preview">Preview</h2>
  </div>
  <Example title="Checkbox group" html={groupHtml.trim()} />

  <div class="govbb-prose">
    <p>
      The Checkbox component lets users select any number of options from a list,
      including none, or toggle a single option on or off. Each checkbox operates
      independently, so selecting one does not affect the others.
    </p>

    <h2 id="when-to-use">When to use this component</h2>
    <p>
      Use checkboxes when users can select more than one option from a list, or need to
      toggle a single option on or off, such as agreeing to terms and conditions.
    </p>

    <h2 id="when-not-to-use">When not to use this component</h2>
    <p>
      Do not use checkboxes when a user must choose exactly one option from a list of
      mutually exclusive choices — use radio buttons instead.
    </p>

    <h2 id="guidance">Guidance</h2>
    <p>
      Group related checkboxes in a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> with a
      <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> that frames the question, for example
      <em>"How would you like to be contacted?"</em>. This associates the options with
      their question for assistive technology.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not pre-select options. Pre-selected checkboxes make it more likely that users
      will not notice they have skipped a question, or will submit an answer they did
      not intend — let users make an active choice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Order options by relevance, listing the most common or recommended choices first.
      Fall back to alphabetical order only when no meaningful priority exists.
    </p>
    <p>
      Make the number of selectable options clear. Do not assume users will infer from
      the visual difference between checkboxes and radio buttons that they can select
      more than one — add a hint such as <em>"Select all that apply"</em> where it
      helps.
    </p>

    <h2 id="single-checkbox">Single checkbox</h2>
    <p>
      Use a standalone checkbox, without a fieldset, for a single opt-in, such as
      accepting terms and conditions.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Example title="Single checkbox" html={singleHtml.trim()} />

  <div class="govbb-prose">
    <h2 id="disabled-checkboxes">Disabled checkboxes</h2>
    <p>
      Disable a checkbox only when an option is genuinely unavailable to the user, and
      say why in the label, for example <em>"(unavailable)"</em>. As with buttons, avoid
      disabling wherever possible — a disabled checkbox gives the user no information
      about what they need to do to enable it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Example title="Disabled checkbox" html={disabledHtml.trim()} />
</ArticleLayout>
